    "available_at": "2026-11-06T09:04:04.278881+00:00",
    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • This Batmobile-Looking Lamborghini Temerario Is on Display at SEMA\nBYD's YANGWANG U9 Xtreme Sets New Production Car Top Speed Record With 312.65 MPH (503.17 KPH) Run\n7 photos\nPhoto: BYD\nFollowing a spectacular crash at more than 224 mph (360 kph), BYD's $3 million electric hypercar eventually made it to Abu Dhabi's Al Dhafra Region in an attempt to finally set a new production car top speed record and smash through that infamous 500 kph (310 mph) barrier.\nAnd smash through it they did, with the YANGWANG U9 Xtreme topping out at 503.17 kph (312.65 mph), surpassing last year's record of 496.22 kph (308.33 mph). In doing so, it secured a total of four Guinness World Records for itself, as well as its tires. The U9 Xtreme is now the fastest production car in the world and the fastest electric production car in the world, while its Giti tires are the fastest EV tires in the world and fastest road car tires in the world.\nThis 500 kph threshold has been pursued by the automotive industry for a while now, with carmakers never quite being able to get there, especially by using conventional internal combustion powertrains. The fact that the U9 Xtreme is putting down a whopping 3,019 ps (2,978 hp) certainly helps.\n\"I always tell people that our engineers are young, bold and maybe a little crazy – but in a good way! And again, we must congratulate them and the whole YANGWANG U9X team for an incredible next step and these astonishing records,\" said BYD executive VP, Stella Li.\n\"Even as the team celebrated the astonishing 496.22km/h (308.22mph) in Germany last year, they were looking through the data and discussing how to take U9X to its next level. 503.17km/h (312.65mph) is a fantastic achievement, and it moves the benchmark for pure electric power even further ahead of where others might dream.\"\nThe path to 500+ kph was a gradual uphill battle. The regular U9 hypercar set its first benchmark back in 2024 by reaching 309 kph (192 mph) at Wuhan Hannah airport in China, before flying to Germany at the ATP Papenburg proving ground in order to lay down a 391.94 kph (243 mph) run - the same facility where the U9 Xtreme made its first appearance at, going directly to 472.41 kph (293.54 mph), before setting a new world production car speed record by hitting 496.22 kph (308.33 mph).\nPhoto: BYD Before this latest record run, YANGWANG engineers made some adjustments to the U9 Xtreme's rear-wing angle in order to increase rear-axle downforce, while also updating the suspension software to increase rebound damping and fine-tuning the tire pressures.\nNaturally, the biggest upgrade was the EV powertrain in the U9X to begin with. It's got so much more power than the standard U9, it's almost laughable. Like they're two different cars. We're talking more than double the power, which is a staggering leap.\nSo, where do we go from here? Well, somebody will just have to come along and break this 503.17 kph record, and I doubt it's going to be Bugatti with the Tourbillon , since the latter \"only\" produces around 1,800 hp. But it's certainly not impossible. Also, the Koenigsegg Jesko Absolut might eventually have a say in this as well. On paper, it could do 500+ kph.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBYD Yangwang U9 Xtreme Yangwang U9 electric electric cars electric vehicles records\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Sergiu Tudose\nSergiu got to experience both American and European car \"scenes\" at an early age (his father drove a Ford Fiesta XR2 supermini in the 80s). After spending over 15 years at local and international auto publications, he's starting to appreciate comfort behind the wheel more than raw power and acceleration. Full profile\nUP NEXT Most Powerful Harley-Davidson Road Glide Gets Even Meaner for the 2027 Bagger World Cup",
